WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.839
The Voyager 1 space probe is the furthest
man-made object from Earth, and the fastest.

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:07.920
But right now, it is moving towards us.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.530
Relatively speaking.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:12.719
This is the control room for NASA’s
Deep Space Network

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:16.810
and the scientists here still listen for the
signals that Voyager is sending.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:21.250
Deep Space Network is a collection of antennas
around the world

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.580
that we use for tracking deep space missions.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.220
We provide commands to the spacecraft,
so y’know, “turn left at the next corner”

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.220
kinds of things, right?

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.500
Little more complicated than that.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.000
Turn the instrument on,
turn the instrument off, software updates.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.200
The most important reason for
sending the thing out there

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.530
is to get the science data back,
and so the instruments are taking

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:44.540
visible pictures, infrared pictures,
measuring particles in the case of Voyager.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.890
We need to get that data back down
and distribute it to the scientists.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.940
The Deep Space Network’s three stations
cover the whole sky.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.840
Any mission more than
30,000 km away from Earth

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.690
is visible to at least one station
all the time.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.940
Geostationary orbit, where we keep most of
our communication satellites,

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.100
is further away than that.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.620
We really have three complexes
around the world:

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:10.050
Madrid in Spain, Goldstone here in California,
and the Canberra complex in Australia.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.130
We typically don’t track things that go
over the pole because

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:16.730
the types of missions that we deal with are
in deep space

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:17.950
and tend to be in the ecliptic.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.720
If you look at the solar system,
where the Sun is,

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.000
and where the planets are that are revolving
around the sun,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.770
they all tend to be more or less in one plane.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.050
That launch along the plane of the solar system,
along the ecliptic,

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.740
is the reason that Voyager is getting closer
to Earth right now.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.770
Yes, Voyager is always moving away from the
sun, but sometimes the Earth’s orbit

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:41.070
means that our planet is travelling in roughly
the same direction only faster.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.120
So we’re catching up.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.180
Of course, we’ll get pulled back in our
elliptical orbit

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.350
so it’ll even out when
we go back the other way.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.400
In terms of checking in with spacecraft,
I think with Voyager for example,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.479
we probably downlink from them
almost every day.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.210
Because again, there’s a lot of good data,
but when you are only coming at 160 bits per second,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.480
it takes a long time to get that data
off the spacecraft.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.260
The signal has to be, in some sense, above
the noise level for us to see it.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.530
You first of all just try to filter the
noise out as much as possible,

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.890
just from a frequency standpoint.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:14.260
My signal occupies this much
spectrum in frequency,

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.500
I don’t want to let in any more noise
than what’s there.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.010
I want to correlate the signal
that’s coming down

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.100
with what I think that signal looks like.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.930
You got to take advantage of what you know
about the signal

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:29.850
and use those characteristics as best you
can to extract it from the noise.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:34.610
Voyager 1 is carrying radioisotope thermoelectric
generators, RTGs.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.920
It’s powered by radioactive decay.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.680
And there’s enough power there to last until
around 2025.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.819
After that, the craft will go dark, carrying
a message forward into interstellar space

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:50.040
for… well, we don’t know where,
or when, or even who.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.400
If there was enough light
being bounced off of it,

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:57.819
you might be able to see it with a telescope,
but it’s so far away, and so dim,

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:00.830
without a radio signal
coming from Voyager at all,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:04.600
there’s no way that we, the DSN,
would be tracking it.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.380
And so I think that’s pretty much the end,
once it goes dark.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.620
Thank you very much to all the team at NASA,
the Jet Propulsion Laboratory,

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:13.260
and here at the Deep Space Network.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.680
Pull down the description for more about them,
and about their missions.

